WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.307
Sparse matrix multiply is a core step of graph

2
00:00:03.372 --> 00:00:05.660
analytics and scientific computing.

3
00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:08.510
The usual method, Gustavson's

4
00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:10.010
works row by row.

5
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.502
For each stored entry of A

6
00:00:12.502 --> 00:00:15.993
in row i and column k, it reads row k of B

7
00:00:15.993 --> 00:00:16.865
scales it

8
00:00:16.865 --> 00:00:19.880
and adds it into row i of the result.

9
00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:22.252
Each stored entry costs one

10
00:00:22.327 --> 00:00:23.750
read of a row of B.

11
00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:27.414
The paper behind this task, from SC 2025

12
00:00:27.414 --> 00:00:30.724
groups neighboring rows of A into clusters

13
00:00:30.799 --> 00:00:32.680
and reads each needed row

14
00:00:32.755 --> 00:00:34.410
of B once per cluster.

15
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.789
To make those rows share columns

16
00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:38.940
it first reorders the rows so that

17
00:00:39.002 --> 00:00:40.600
similar rows sit together.

18
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.245
Its best scheme is one point three

19
00:00:43.313 --> 00:00:45.270
nine times faster on average.

20
00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.724
The task uses the simplest one

21
00:00:47.724 --> 00:00:50.575
fixed clusters of eight consecutive rows

22
00:00:50.575 --> 00:00:52.340
after a fixed reordering.

23
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.582
Here is a matrix of our own

24
00:00:54.641 --> 00:00:56.125
the five-point stencil of

25
00:00:56.185 --> 00:00:57.550
an eight by eight grid

26
00:00:57.609 --> 00:00:59.330
with two hundred eighty-eight

27
00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:00.280
stored entries.

28
00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:01.400
Row by row

29
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.550
that is two hundred eighty-eight reads of B.

30
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:06.801
Eight at a time in grid order

31
00:01:06.801 --> 00:01:08.765
it is one hundred seventy-six

32
00:01:08.765 --> 00:01:11.300
because neighboring rows share columns.

33
00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.526
Shuffled rows share fewer

34
00:01:13.526 --> 00:01:15.330
two hundred twenty-nine.

35
00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.595
A simple reordering wins it back and more

36
00:01:18.595 --> 00:01:20.400
at one hundred sixty-six.

37
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.883
In the task, the order is given

38
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.750
the agent writes the format

39
00:01:24.817 --> 00:01:25.950
and the multiply.

40
00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:28.514
For each group of eight rows

41
00:01:28.582 --> 00:01:30.429
the format keeps one sorted

42
00:01:30.498 --> 00:01:31.797
list of column ids

43
00:01:31.797 --> 00:01:33.576
with eight values per id

44
00:01:33.576 --> 00:01:35.970
and zero where a row has no entry.

45
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.758
The multiply reads each row of B once

46
00:01:38.822 --> 00:01:41.132
and updates all eight lanes of every

47
00:01:41.196 --> 00:01:42.800
output column it reaches.

48
00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:45.720
One warning: the task fixes the order

49
00:01:45.789 --> 00:01:47.387
and rules of additions

50
00:01:47.387 --> 00:01:49.054
so an ordinary product

51
00:01:49.054 --> 00:01:52.110
correct as mathematics, can fail the check.

52
00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:54.879
Each submission multiplies whole

53
00:01:54.947 --> 00:01:56.589
matrices from mechanics

54
00:01:56.589 --> 00:01:59.530
stencils and graphs, on sixty CPU threads

55
00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:01.650
timed from loading to the end.

56
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.699
To pass, on every matrix

57
00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:06.417
the time must be below zero point eight

58
00:02:06.485 --> 00:02:08.320
five times the reference's.

59
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:10.500
Pause and predict.

60
00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.481
A submission is exactly one point one

61
00:02:13.552 --> 00:02:15.970
five times faster on every matrix.

62
00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:17.440
Does it pass?

63
00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:21.260
No.

64
00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.555
Its time is about zero point eight

65
00:02:23.623 --> 00:02:25.310
seven of the reference's.

66
00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:27.380
It needs a speed-up above

67
00:02:27.449 --> 00:02:29.100
one point one seven six.

68
00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:31.283
And a run three times faster

69
00:02:31.348 --> 00:02:32.526
on seven matrices

70
00:02:32.526 --> 00:02:35.406
but only one point one times on the eighth

71
00:02:35.406 --> 00:02:38.090
scores seventy-two point six, and fails.

72
00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.292
Twelve of sixteen models passed

73
00:02:41.292 --> 00:02:44.210
the third-easiest of the forty-eight tasks.

74
00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.806
Claude Opus 5.5 scored sixty-six point nine

75
00:02:47.806 --> 00:02:50.240
against fifty for the reference.

76
00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:52.707
It counts the output columns first

77
00:02:52.768 --> 00:02:55.066
adds each product into all eight lanes

78
00:02:55.127 --> 00:02:56.820
with one vector instruction

79
00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.393
in the reference's order

80
00:02:58.393 --> 00:03:00.510
and runs the heavy clusters first.

81
00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:03.075
Kimi K3 scored fifty-six point

82
00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:06.251
seven and still failed: faster on average

83
00:03:06.251 --> 00:03:09.500
but not by fifteen percent on every matrix.

84
00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.167
That ends the Architecture lecture

85
00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:13.360
with three lessons.

86
00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:16.270
Find the bottleneck first

87
00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:18.436
KAMI waits on memory

88
00:03:18.436 --> 00:03:21.390
and SpGEMM on the reads of B.

89
00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.686
A margin changes the target

90
00:03:23.755 --> 00:03:26.466
fifteen percent faster means a speed-up

91
00:03:26.535 --> 00:03:28.620
above one point one seven six.

92
00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:31.850
And a tuned reference is hard to beat

93
00:03:31.926 --> 00:03:34.200
all sixteen RUNLTS runs stayed

94
00:03:34.275 --> 00:03:36.170
within two percent of it.

95
00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:37.699
As an exercise

96
00:03:37.699 --> 00:03:41.000
try clusters of four and of sixteen rows.

97
00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:43.274
The interactive notes run every

98
00:03:43.336 --> 00:03:44.950
toy model in your browser.

99
00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:48.500
Next time: Lecture 4, Network.
